# Third-party components

## jrt-fs.jar — OpenJDK

`public/java-playground/jrt-fs.jar` is taken unmodified from an OpenJDK 17
distribution (Eclipse Temurin). It is the reader that lets a compiler open a JDK
module image (`lib/modules`).

It is needed because CheerpJ's Java 17 runtime ships the module image but not this
reader, and its `/lt` mount is read-only — so the playground assembles a usable JDK
in its own writable directory at run time. See
`src/lib/java-playground/constants.ts` for the mechanics.

OpenJDK is licensed under the **GNU General Public License, version 2, with the
Classpath Exception**. The Classpath Exception permits linking with this file
without extending the GPL to the linking work.

- License: https://openjdk.org/legal/gplv2+ce.html
- Source: https://github.com/openjdk/jdk17u

## Eclipse Compiler for Java (ECJ)

`public/java-playground/ecj-*.jar` is downloaded from Maven Central at build time
by `scripts/fetch-java-playground-assets.mjs` and is **not** stored in this
repository. It is served to the browser as a static asset, unmodified.

ECJ is licensed under the **Eclipse Public License 2.0**.

- License: https://www.eclipse.org/legal/epl-2.0/
- Source: https://github.com/eclipse-jdt/eclipse.jdt.core

## CheerpJ

The Java runtime is loaded at page load from Leaning Technologies' CDN
(`cjrtnc.leaningtech.com`). Nothing from CheerpJ is redistributed here.

- https://cheerpj.com

## Networking material from FSC Open Docs

The two pages in `src/content/frc/frc-networking/` (*The Robot Network* and
*Troubleshooting a Robot Connection*) are adapted from the Networking page in
[FSC Open Docs](https://github.com/firstsouthcarolina/FSCOpenDocs), written by FIRST
South Carolina and contributors including this team's mentors, whose prose is licensed
**CC BY-SA 4.0**. ShareAlike requires adaptations to carry the same licence, so those two
pages do, and each carries an Origin box saying so.
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

## Authorship by section

Tools, Java, Practice and Kit Bot were written by FRC 4451 for this site and carry no
third-party licence. Java and Practice take direct inspiration from the University of
South Carolina's CSCE 145 and CSCE 146 (https://www.cse.sc.edu/~shephejj/csce145/ and
.../csce146/). Kit Bot was written by the mentors from FRC 4864's build of *Skorpion*.
FRC combines Mantik's original lessons with FRC 4451's rewrites and additions; the PID
Tuning Practice group uses Mantik's practice project and tutorial videos. The base of the
Resources catalog is Mantik's work. Mantik's FTC lessons and browser PID simulator were
removed from this fork in September 2026.

## Upstream repositories

This site is a fork of [itkan-robotics/mantik](https://github.com/itkan-robotics/mantik).
The Java playground under `src/lib/java-playground/`, `src/components/java-playground/`
and `scripts/java*` is ported from
[FRC3476/mantik-orange](https://github.com/FRC3476/mantik-orange), itself a fork of
that project.

**Neither upstream publishes a licence yet.** A licensing
request is open against itkan-robotics/mantik and has not yet been decided. Until
both licences are actually published, no licence can be granted for the parts of
this repository derived from them, so this fork carries no root `LICENSE` file. The
licences named above apply only to the third-party components and the FSC-derived
content they describe.

- Licensing request to itkan-robotics/mantik:
  https://github.com/itkan-robotics/mantik/issues/32
